WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.819
This is an observatory that’s trying to
measure gravitational waves that are distortions

00:00:05.819 --> 00:00:11.960
in space-time produced by black holes out
there in the universe.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:17.050
We measure these distortions with lasers going
back and forth between mirrors, measuring

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:23.949
distances that will change very, very slightly
due to these black holes sending us messages

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:27.050
from millions of years away.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:32.840
When the theory of relativity was created,
it said that masses in general — and black

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:38.580
holes because they’re very compact — distort
space-time and produce these waves that travel

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.070
away at the speed of light.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:48.011
We want to measure here on Earth what happens
when those black holes were born — when

00:00:48.011 --> 00:00:54.070
supernovas exploded, when neutron stars collided
to form a black hole.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:59.750
And we want to detect the effect of those
astronomical events here on Earth.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.600
It’s something that has never been done
before.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.180
We know gravitational waves exist.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:10.220
They are predicted by the theory of relativity,
but we have also seen the effects of gravitational

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.350
waves from systems that loose energy.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.240
But we have never measured them directly here
on Earth.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.390
That’s the new window to the universe we
want to open.

